#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447c54fa-843c-555a-a73c-b8697bb862d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353693} {:answer/id #uuid "4172aa23-ca1c-5cf4-9548-e5e14b9e6e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353694} {:answer/id #uuid "51325632-f660-5384-a161-cdc54cda055f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353695} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7a3c2d-ae5b-5d8c-98c3-8d74e2f07cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353696} {:answer/id #uuid "fc14a16b-48f3-52df-9e64-bf329dea3118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "447c54fa-843c-555a-a73c-b8697bb862d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353693} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd3ec06-fb08-5207-a992-08a33da51ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12ec4938-eb26-5d97-a9e5-0e654fa11c89" :question/math-academy-id "q-115836" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptote at $x=2.$  The root and the asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+3$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive in the interval $(-\\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afcc0710-03b0-59a6-8454-cc02b92b035b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353700} {:answer/id #uuid "29260512-10cb-5e52-829c-dcf37317766b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353701} {:answer/id #uuid "4981a775-126d-5c53-925a-82fd163b6b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353702} {:answer/id #uuid "2958ddfe-2360-58bf-a816-b61bf0230450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353703} {:answer/id #uuid "83bd6d6a-1f8d-5139-93d7-1f4f53b617f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83bd6d6a-1f8d-5139-93d7-1f4f53b617f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353704} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad0de1d-f54f-5372-aceb-bd35325bc8ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab49cabc-074b-5c94-8d39-a16e173ce902" :question/math-academy-id "q-115978" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-5x}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-5x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{x(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=0$ and $x=5.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "513bfde9-209d-55d3-a6c1-d14b536b27b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353707} {:answer/id #uuid "84645be2-e821-5be0-9ad5-03dd678f64f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513bfde9-209d-55d3-a6c1-d14b536b27b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353707} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b417f02-47a0-543a-b504-73b3606d4037" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802765bd-721d-5468-b027-cdd97a48c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802765bd-721d-5468-b027-cdd97a48c156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353710} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8b8fbf-ce1b-5c5a-9f7c-bc0d6baf5792" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d10e739-cb17-5dc8-b70b-1c5ef4b5b6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d10e739-cb17-5dc8-b70b-1c5ef4b5b6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353712} :answer-field/id #uuid "309d386d-1d2a-53ec-9216-5931fd233ebf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bcd6c69-554f-5b0a-95f0-12381ca7ad79" :question/math-academy-id "q-324342" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality  $\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Since the function equals zero at $x=-5$ and at $x=2,$ we include these endpoints in the solution.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in [\\,-5,-1\\,)⋃[\\,2,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Inequality by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892b0bcb-f0d2-51ce-b4af-050e7fadfce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892b0bcb-f0d2-51ce-b4af-050e7fadfce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353715} :answer-field/id #uuid "48058ad1-56a4-57b8-b249-71edecc1f204" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a0eada-63a2-5faf-9e19-5a92a2458c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35a0eada-63a2-5faf-9e19-5a92a2458c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353717} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad23a4d-afdd-5836-bb20-dbc43002af85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4463617b-be17-5773-9867-77da8312f01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4463617b-be17-5773-9867-77da8312f01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353719} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa776aa8-b93e-5cbb-bd79-a5074b012dfc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff8c41fd-4007-5e0e-bd90-44213e7e6540" :question/math-academy-id "q-326139" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+15}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+3)(x+5)}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=-3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-3),\\,(-3,-2),\\,(-2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-3)$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $[-5,-3]$ and $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in [-5,-3\\,]\\cup (-2,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Rational Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f17dbf13-bbc2-5bcb-983e-5f6e55ab3d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353722} {:answer/id #uuid "91ffab05-989b-555e-bbd5-86650e799737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353723} {:answer/id #uuid "f7921c35-4478-5e2e-b0fc-4a5a16417732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353724} {:answer/id #uuid "075c5c4b-f02b-5492-9181-99bec44999f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353725} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9f2f70-a403-5d28-ad58-6fc5358d09aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7921c35-4478-5e2e-b0fc-4a5a16417732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353724} :answer-field/id #uuid "385c1d9c-97e2-5bf9-b57a-f1c446d37b84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3ce9d28-58a9-55ff-b359-ad7f316a7c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38515" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2).$"}]}
